          </div>
          <p>
            This application is constructed using client-side vanilla JavaScript coordinated with <strong>PouchDB 8.0+</strong> and stylized with <strong>Tailwind CSS</strong>. PouchDB was selected as the client driver because it provides an identical API to CouchDB and natively implements the CouchDB Replication Protocol over HTTP.
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 pt-2">
            <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-3 rounded-xl border border-slate-800">
              <span class="font-semibold text-slate-200 block text-xs mb-1">Underlying Storage Drivers</span>
              <p class="text-slate-400 text-[11px]">
                In modern desktop and mobile browsers, PouchDB binds directly to the <strong>IndexedDB driver</strong> (or WebSQL in legacy engines). IndexedDB grants transactional, asynchronous, durable local persistence that operates fully decoupled from network connectivity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-slate-900/80 p-3 rounded-xl border border-slate-800">
              <span class="font-semibold text-slate-200 block text-xs mb-1">CouchDB Replication Protocol</span>
              <p class="text-slate-400 text-[11px]">
                Communication is driven by CouchDB's <code class="text-indigo-300">_changes</code> feed. Rather than issuing full database dumps, the replication driver computes sequence checkpoints (<code class="text-indigo-300">since: 'now'</code>), streaming only delta changes bidirectionally with automatic retry back-offs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 2: Schema A Implementation -->
        <div class="bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-700/60 p-5 rounded-2xl space-y-3">
          <div class="flex items-center space-x-2 text-amber-400 font-bold text-sm">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-database"></i>
            <h4>2. Schema_A Design and Document Structure</h4>
          </div>
          <p>
            In accordance with the specification, documents strictly adhere to <strong>Schema_A</strong>:
          </p>
          <pre class="bg-slate-950 p-3 rounded-xl font-mono text-[11px] text-emerald-400 border border-slate-800 overflow-x-auto">
{
  "_id": "mov-inception-2010",        // Movie_ID (CouchDB primary key)
  "_rev": "1-a1b2c3d4...",           // CouchDB revision identifier
  "Title": "Inception",              // String
  "Actors": ["Leonardo DiCaprio", "Elliot Page", "Tom Hardy"], // Array of Strings
  "Genres": ["Action", "Sci-Fi", "Thriller"]                  // Array of Strings
}</pre>
          <p class="text-slate-400 text-[11px]">
            Input strings in the form fields are tokenized and cleaned via <code class="text-slate-200 font-mono">val.split(',').map(s =&gt; s.trim()).filter(Boolean)</code> to ensure database consistency as array lists.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 3: Scenario Evaluation Analysis -->
        <div class="bg-slate-800/60 border border-slate-700/60 p-5 rounded-2xl space-y-3">
          <div class="flex items-center space-x-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold text-sm">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-list-check"></i>
            <h4>3. Evaluation of Demonstrated Scenarios</h4>
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-2 text-[11px] text-slate-300">
            <p><strong>Scenario 1 (Create Offline):</strong> When <code class="text-slate-200">navigator.onLine = false</code>, PouchDB executes `put()` or `post()` against the IndexedDB storage layer. The transaction commits locally and yields a generation-1 revision (e.g. `1-xxx`). The UI updates immediately with zero latency.</p>
            <p><strong>Scenario 2 (Update Offline):</strong> Updating an existing document offline requires reading the current `_rev` and incrementing the generation tree (`2-yyy`). The update remains queued locally in the changes feed waiting for sync resumption.</p>
            <p><strong>Scenario 3 (Delete Offline):</strong> Deleting a movie offline attaches a tombstone attribute <code class="text-slate-200 font-mono">_deleted: true</code> with an updated revision. The document is filtered out of standard queries, but preserved in the revision log to replicate the deletion outward.</p>
            <p><strong>Scenario 4 (Synchronising after Reconnecting):</strong> Upon reconnection, <code class="text-slate-200 font-mono">PouchDB.sync(local, remote, {live: true, retry: true})</code> reactivates. The replication handler fires <code class="text-cyan-300">active</code>, <code class="text-cyan-300">change</code>, and <code class="text-cyan-300">paused</code> events, transmitting pending revisions and bringing remote CouchDB and local PouchDB into parity.</p>
            <p><strong>Scenario 5 (Conflict Updates & Multi-Master DAG):</strong> When two nodes modify the same revision concurrently while partitioned, both generate conflicting `2-xxx` leaf revisions. CouchDB does <em>not</em> throw an unrecoverable error; it builds a revision tree (Directed Acyclic Graph) and deterministically picks a winner. Both branches exist until resolved by fetching <code class="text-purple-300">{conflicts: true}</code> and deleting the rejected revision.</p>
          </div>
